      "reasons": [
        "The packet discusses “fibromyalgia” and refers to a “pathophysiological concept of enhanced central pain processing,” but it does not define the term “central pain” in general (e.g., as arising from altered processing in the brain’s pain-related systems rather than tissue damage).",
        "The definition’s statement that central pain is used to describe “chronic, stimulus-independent pain” is only indirectly related: the packet contrasts acute experimentally evoked pain paradigms with “chronic, stimulus-independent pain” in the context of fibromyalgia, but it does not establish that this is the standard meaning of the term “central pain.”",
        "The definition claims central pain “may reflect enhanced central pain processing,” but the packet does not provide enough definitional support to treat this wording as the requested meaning of “central pain” itself."
